---
/**
 * /average-ring-size — the "is my size normal / what should I guess" page.
 *
 * Why this page exists: three >1000/mo keywords (`average ring size for women`,
 * `average ring size for men`, `average ring size`) plus roughly ten more at
 * >100, with `average ring size for women` measured at KD 0 and a DR 18 page
 * taking 3.2K traffic in the top 5. The homepage's one-paragraph "Is your size
 * normal?" section cannot rank for thirteen keywords across two genders, so it
 * shrinks to a summary and the detail lives here. See RESEARCH.md, 28 Aug pass.
 *
 * MUST NOT become a conversion chart. /ring-size-chart answers "what converts
 * to what"; this page answers "what is typical". The only sizes printed here
 * are the averages and the bands around them.
 *
 * ⚠️ Every other page on this site prints numbers computed from a published
 * standard. This one cannot: no standard governs "average ring size" and no
 * primary source exists — see the comment on AVERAGE_US_SIZE in
 * src/data/ringSizes.ts. Saying so is the entire differentiator. Every
 * competitor states 6 and 9 as fact.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import FaqSection from '../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { NOINDEX_SITE, SITE_URL } from '../config';
import { AVERAGE_US_SIZE, diameterFromUs, fromDiameter, CHART_ROWS } from '../data/ringSizes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, AVERAGE_FAQS, faqPageSchema } from '../data/faq';

const PAGE_URL = `${SITE_URL}/average-ring-size`;

/* Derived, never typed — the averages themselves are the only unsourced input,
   and everything downstream of them is computed. */
const women = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(AVERAGE_US_SIZE.women));
const men = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(AVERAGE_US_SIZE.men));

const womenLow = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(4));
const womenHigh = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(8.5));
const menLow = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(8));
const menHigh = fromDiameter(diameterFromUs(13));

const sold = CHART_ROWS.filter((r) => r.usNumeric !== null);
const smallest = sold[0];
const largest = sold[sold.length - 1];

/* One US size is this much finger. Used to show how little separates the
   averages from everything either side of them. */
const oneSizeMm = diameterFromUs(7) - diameterFromUs(6);

const jsonLd = [
  faqPageSchema(AVERAGE_FAQS),
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Ring Sizer', url: SITE_URL },
    { name: 'Average Ring Size', url: PAGE_URL },
  ]),
];
---
<Layout
  title="Average Ring Size for Women and Men | Ring Size Tool"
  description="The average ring size is US 6 for women and US 9 for men. Where those figures come from, how wide the real spread is, and why no standard governs either."
  noindex={NOINDEX_SITE}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 sm:py-12">

    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-sm text-text-faint">
      <a href="/" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">Ring Sizer</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Average ring size
    </nav>

    <header class="mt-6">
      <h1 class="max-w-[20ch] text-[clamp(2.25rem,5.5vw,3.5rem)]">
        Average <span class="text-accent">ring size</span> </h1>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[60ch] text-lg text-text-muted">
        US&nbsp;{women.us} for women and US&nbsp;{men.us} for men. Those are the figures every
        jeweller quotes, and they are worth rather less than they look.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-6 border-l-2 border-accent py-1 pl-4 text-sm text-text-muted">
        Every other number on this site is computed from a published standard. These two are not,
        because no standard governs them and no primary source exists. What that means in practice
        is set out below — no other page answering this question tells you.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- ── Women ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Query: "average ring size for women" (>1000, KD 0), "what is the
         average ring size for a woman", "average ring size women". -->
    <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="women">
      <h2 id="women" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">Average ring size for women</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The commonly cited average is <strong class="text-text">US&nbsp;{women.us}</strong> — an
        inner diameter of <span class="tabular">{women.diameterMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm</span>, a
        circumference of <span class="tabular">{women.circumferenceMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm</span>,
        and UK&nbsp;{women.uk} or EU&nbsp;{women.eu}.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Most women who buy rings fall between about US&nbsp;4 and US&nbsp;8½, which is <span class="tabular">{womenLow.diameterMm.toFixed(1)}</span> to <span class="tabular">{womenHigh.diameterMm.toFixed(1)}&nbsp;mm</span> — a span of roughly <span class="tabular">{(womenHigh.diameterMm - womenLow.diameterMm).toFixed(0)}&nbsp;mm</span> of finger.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Men ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Query: "average ring size for men" (>1000), "mens average ring size",
         "what is the average ring size for a man". Men are under-served across
         three separate keyword clusters; this is one of them. -->
    <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="men">
      <h2 id="men" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">Average ring size for men</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The commonly cited average is <strong class="text-text">US&nbsp;{men.us}</strong> — an inner
        diameter of <span class="tabular">{men.diameterMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm</span>, a
        circumference of <span class="tabular">{men.circumferenceMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm</span>, and
        UK&nbsp;{men.uk} or EU&nbsp;{men.eu}.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The typical band runs about US&nbsp;8 to US&nbsp;13 — <span class="tabular">{menLow.diameterMm.toFixed(1)}</span> to <span class="tabular">{menHigh.diameterMm.toFixed(1)}&nbsp;mm</span>. It is wider than the
        women's band, and men's bands are also more often wide-profile, which makes a ring fit
        tighter at the same nominal size.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-surface shadow-xs">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[32rem] text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">
            Commonly cited average ring sizes and typical ranges for women and men, with inner
            diameter in millimetres.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-border bg-surface-sunk/80">
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-text">&nbsp;</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-text">Most common</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-text">Typical range</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent">Inner diameter</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="tabular divide-y divide-border/60">
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3.5 font-semibold text-text">Women</th>
              <td class="px-4 py-3.5 font-medium text-text">US {women.us}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3.5 text-text-muted">US 4 – 8½</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3.5 font-mono text-accent">{`${womenLow.diameterMm.toFixed(1)} – ${womenHigh.diameterMm.toFixed(1)} mm`}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3.5 font-semibold text-text">Men</th>
              <td class="px-4 py-3.5 font-medium text-text">US {men.us}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3.5 text-text-muted">US 8 – 13</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3.5 font-mono text-accent">{`${menLow.diameterMm.toFixed(1)} – ${menHigh.diameterMm.toFixed(1)} mm`}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Provenance ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The differentiator. Every competitor states 6 and 9 as fact. -->
    <section class="mt-16" aria-labelledby="source">
      <h2 id="source" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">Where these numbers come from</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Ring sizes themselves are standardised. ISO&nbsp;8653 defines the EU scale, JIS&nbsp;S&nbsp;4700
        defines Japan&rsquo;s, BS&nbsp;EN&nbsp;28653 defines the UK letters. Every size on this site is
        computed from one of those. <strong class="text-text">The average is not.</strong>
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        We went looking for a primary source in August 2026 and did not find one. What exists is
        jewellers publishing their own sales figures, and other pages repeating them. The attribution
        you see most often is to the Gemological Institute of America, but the pages citing it cite
        each other rather than a publication, and the supporting details — a database of ten thousand
        customers, a quarter of American women wearing a six — are not checkable by anyone outside
        the company making the claim.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        There has never been a census of finger sizes. What these figures actually describe is
        <strong class="text-text">what jewellers stock and sell</strong> — ring buyers, not people.
        Engagement rings skew the sample young and female; wedding bands skew it differently again.
        A size {smallest.us} and a size {largest.us} are both entirely ordinary.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] rounded-xl bg-surface-sunk p-4 text-sm text-text-faint">
        We print them anyway, because the question gets asked constantly and refusing to answer
        helps nobody. But they are a rough central tendency from retail, not a measurement, and
        nothing on this page rounds to a decimal place they could support.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── What it is worth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Query: "is ring size 7 big for a woman", "is a size 8 ring big for a
         woman", "is ring size 6 small" — all in the PAA for this keyword. -->
    <section class="mt-16" aria-labelledby="worth">
      <h2 id="worth" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">How much the average tells you</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Less than the number of digits suggests. One full US size is <span class="tabular">{oneSizeMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm</span> of diameter — about the
        thickness of a stack of ten sheets of paper. The whole women&rsquo;s band from US&nbsp;4 to
        US&nbsp;8½ spans a finger difference you could cover with a fingernail.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        So <strong class="text-text">US&nbsp;7 is not a large ring for a woman</strong>: it is one
        size above the commonest, and two below the commonest men&rsquo;s. US&nbsp;6 is not small.
        Both sit in the middle of what every jeweller keeps in stock. If you came here to find out
        whether your size is unusual, it almost certainly is not.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Buying blind ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="mt-16" aria-labelledby="guess">
      <h2 id="guess" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">If you are buying without asking</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        This is why most people search for the average, and it is the case it serves worst. Buying
        US&nbsp;{women.us} for someone whose size you do not know is a coin flip on a ring that has
        to be resized — which is possible for most plain bands and impossible for eternity rings and
        some tension settings.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Borrowing a ring they already wear takes two minutes and is genuinely accurate. There is a
        <a href="/how-to-measure-ring-size-at-home#without" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">short guide to sizing someone else&rsquo;s finger without telling them</a>,
        ranked by how well each approach actually works. If you truly cannot, guess the average and
        buy somewhere that resizes free.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-8">
        <a
          href="/"
          class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-full bg-accent px-6 font-semibold text-on-accent no-underline transition-colors hover:bg-accent-hover"
        >
          Measure a real size instead &rarr;
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>


    <FaqSection faqs={AVERAGE_FAQS} heading="Average ring size questions" id="faq" />

  </div>
</Layout>
